    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d934">

                <pb facs="1211" n="1201" sameAs="frus:frus1946v10/pg1201"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/1–246: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Robertson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1946-01-02T13:00:00+07:00">January 2, 1946—1
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received January 3—3:38 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>7. According to telegram dated December 30 from Ward,<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d934-f1"/> Chiang Ching-kuo<anchor n="3" xml:id="d934-f2"/> arrived
                    Tihwa December 26 and departed for Moscow December 28. (Embassy’s 2232, December
                    28 to Dept, repeated Moscow.<anchor n="4" xml:id="d934-f3"/>) Ward says
                    negotiations are proceeding with rebels; that Liu<anchor n="5" xml:id="d934-f4"
                    /> is confident of settlement and feels it may be soon, although (sent to Dept
                    as 7; repeated to Moscow as 2) some local Chinese have taken umbrage at two new
                    rebel desiderata: (1) that troops brought in to put down rebellion be withdrawn
                    after settlement and (2) that secret police be disbanded.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Robertson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d934-f1" n="2" xml:id="d934-f1-content">
                <p>Robert S. Ward, Consul at Tihwa.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d934-f2" n="3" xml:id="d934-f2-content">
                <p>Son of Generalissimo Chiang Kai-shek, President of the National Government of the
                    Republic of China.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d934-f3" n="4" xml:id="d934-f3-content">
